\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}
\section{Vibration Model}
Consider a single-degree-of-freedom oscillator with mass $m$, damping $c$, and stiffness $k$. The displacement $x(t)$ obeys
\begin{equation}
m \ddot{x} + c \dot{x} + k x = F(t)
\label{eq:response}
\end{equation}
The natural frequency is $\omega_n = \sqrt{k/m}$ and the damping ratio is $\zeta = c / (2 m \omega_n)$. When the driving frequency approaches $\omega_n$, resonance occurs and the amplitude of $x(t)$ grows until damping balances the input energy. In practice we estimate $\zeta$ from the logarithmic decrement of free vibration, then verify the stiffness $k$ against static deflection tests.
\end{document}
